  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe core idea is to optimize a step function h:[0,2]\u2192[0,1] with integral 1 to minimize the maximum overlap with its shifted versions. We adopt a multi-resolution strategy using gradient-free evolution strategies (GES) to efficiently explore the space of candidate solutions. The main limitations are the high dimensionality and non-convexity, which we address by:\n1. Exploiting grid symmetries to reduce search space\n2. Using linear interpolation to transfer solutions between resolutions\n3. Applying an evolution strategy with adaptive mutation rates\nThe key innovation is using a constrained optimization approach with repair mechanisms ensuring feasible solutions throughout the search.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Representation: h as function on [0,2] sampled at n points\n- Constraints: h_i \u2208 [0,1], \u2211h_i * dx = 1 (dx=2/n)\n- Objective: Minimize max_k \u27e8h, s\u27e9_dx where s_i = 1 - h_{(i+k) mod (n-1)}\n- Implementation: Uses finite difference derivatives and line search\n- Solver: Constrained optimization by projecting solutions back to feasible set\n- Repair: Adjusts values to maintain sum constraint via volume preservation\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialize population with:\n   - Linearly upsampled parent solution\n   - Piecewise triangular shape\n   - Random feasibility adjustments\n2. For each candidate:\n   a. Project to [0,1]^n bounds\n   b. Adjust sum to target\n   c. Compute objective via correlation analysis\nd. Use constrained optimization with adaptive step size\ne. Maintain population diversity\nf. Select next generation by fitness ranking\n\nSearch choices:\n- Resolution doubling: n=200,400,800 (if time allows)\n- Population size: auto-adjusted by time budget\n- Mutation rate: starts at 0.1 and adaptively decreases\n- Time allocation: 60% to initial, 30% to continuation\n\nComputational budget:\n- Dominant cost: O(n^2) per candidate evaluation\n- Time estimate: 30 sec for n=200 (target duration)\n- Early termination: If continuous improvement stalls\n\nValidation:\n- For final solution: Verify sum constraint |\u2211h_i - n/2| \u2264 \u03b5\n- Check finite C5 value\n- Re-compute C5 via correlation to match evaluator\n- Ensure all values in [0,1]\n\nReturn contract:\n- Returns (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n- Times out: n_points = 200, strategy adaptive\n- Parent transfer: handled via upsampling",
